## Diagram 1: Three Pinned Values, Nothing Else

Placement: After the "## The Three Pinned Values" heading, before the subject format block
Alt text: A keyless trust in the customer's cloud admits a token only when the issuer, the audience, and the subject all match; Planton cloud accounts, shared secrets, and other connections are outside the trust
Style: technical

### Prompt:
Create a diagram of a single lock in the center labeled "Your cloud's trust", with three keyholes arranged vertically on its face.

Key elements:
- Keyhole 1 labeled "Issuer: https://planton.ai"
- Keyhole 2 labeled "Audience: sts.amazonaws.com / provider resource name / api://AzureADTokenExchange"
- Keyhole 3 labeled "Subject: planton:v1:org:acme:conn:acme-aws-prod:provider:aws"
- On the left, a token labeled "Token signed by Planton" with three teeth that match the three keyholes, connected to the lock by an arrow labeled "all three must match"
- On the right, outside the lock, three grayed-out items each with a small "not trusted" mark: "Planton cloud account", "Shared secret / external ID", "Another connection's token"
- Below the lock, a small element labeled "Signature checked against https://planton.ai/.well-known/jwks.json"

Visual style:
- Dark background (#1f2937) with light text
- Blue accent color (#60a5fa) for the token and the issuer keyhole
- Amber (#f59e0b) for the lock
- Gray (#9ca3af) for the not-trusted items
- Clean, minimal, professional
- No decorative elements

Context: This diagram explains, to a security reviewer, that a keyless connection's trust admits exactly one connection's token from one issuer, and nothing else.
